  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nShowers and storms have developed overnight along an east-west front\nthat is across northern Indiana into west central Ohio will continue\nthrough much of the morning. This is along the low level jet axis. \nWith precipitable water around 2 inches and warm cloud depth over 4 \nkm, very efficient rainfall process are occurring. This will lead to \nthe potential for streaks of high rainfall amounts that could lead to\nflash flooding. \n\nLow level winds will veer to the west after daybreak which will\nreduce the convergence along the front. But a short wave exiting \nIowa will move across the region giving some mid level support. It \nstill looks like the primary threat will be through the morning hours\nwith activity diminishing quite a bit heading into the afternoon. \n\nGiven observational trends as well as trends in short term guidance,\nhave opted to trim the southern extent of the watch. This may be on\nthe conservative side as it appears that the main concern will \nremain across west central Ohio, but still wanted to leave a buffer \nin case activity sags a bit further south. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nWith no change in airmass, expect temperatures and dew points to be \nsimilar to yesterday unless clouds remain thick enough along the I-70\ncorridor to keep temperatures a little lower there. The end result \nis that heat indices will be similar with values approaching 100 \nacross southern counties.\n\nThe front will move south tonight and stall south of the area on\nFriday. This along with increased cloud cover will result in lower\ntemperatures on Friday. Somewhat drier air will get into northern\ncounties, but it will still be humid in the south. There will be some\nrecovery over the weekend, although there is some spread in guidance\nwith how quickly the front washes out and southerly flow returns. \n\nAnother cold front will move through later Sunday. Temperatures will\ngradually trend cooler each day heading into mid week by which time\nreadings area forecast closer to normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3)\nFront extending across the northern part of the area today will move\nsouth tonight. A weak short wave will ride down the front side of \nthe retrograding mid level high tonight. Much of the guidance is\nindicating that this will result in showers and storms increasing in\ncoverage tonight in the vicinity of the boundary, especially after \n06Z. There will still be very high precipitable water and a deep warm\ncloud layer, so cannot rule out some locally heavy rainfall. 0Z HEFS\nis a bit more bullish than the REFS in showing some higher rainfall \ntotals.\n\nThere should be a break in rainfall potential from Friday afternoon\ninto Saturday morning. But then the southern edge of the westerlies\nsag into the region which will allow more mid level energy to move\nthrough. Meanwhile, the front will lift back north bringing a return\nof the very moist environment. More showers and storms with at least\nsome heavy rain potential will be possible through the weekend until\nthe front passes south again and eventually gets further from the\narea.",
